+\section{Galaxy and group on one foreground domain: a fixed-input test}
+\label{sec:he0435-unified-foreground}
+Can the retained R1 equation predict the lens field from the galaxy and
+its surroundings, without fitting an extra lens potential? We test a
+restricted version of that question. We put the main galaxy and its
+spectroscopic group in one foreground field solve and propagate the
+result through the separate, more distant G1 plane. No image positions
+or time delays are used to set field parameters; the observed image
+positions are used only to evaluate the resulting map. This complements
+the fitted-model tests above and is not an additional fit.
+
+\paragraph{Source specification and field solve.}
+At $z_1=0.4546$ we deproject the circularized main-galaxy stellar
+S\'ersic profile ($n=3.51$, $R_e=3.984$ kpc,
+$M_\star=10^{11.1}M_\odot$) from published imaging and stellar-population
+inputs~\cite{Zahedy2016HE}. We regularize the inner density by replacing
+$r$ with $\sqrt{r^2+(0.5\,\mathrm{kpc})^2}$ and renormalize to the stated
+stellar mass. The approximately same-redshift group has a reported
+projected offset of $67.1''$ and a dispersion estimate of
+$470\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$~\cite{Sluse2017H0LiCOWII}. We model it as
+the difference of two softened isothermal density profiles of scales
+25 and 761 kpc and place the group offset in the sky plane. Those
+scales and the line-of-sight placement are model choices, not a measured
+three-dimensional density. The rear $z_2=0.7821$ G1 is a separate galaxy,
+represented by a circularized bulge and disk with a common
+stellar mass-to-light ratio and total stellar mass $10^{10.9}M_\odot$
+from the published inputs~\cite{Kochanek2006HE,Wong2017HE}.
+The foreground excludes a primary dark halo and any additional fitted
+external convergence or shear; the group proxy still contributes its
+ordinary gravity. This is therefore not a baryon-only test of the entire
+line of sight.
+
+We solve the static R1 scalar equation for the summed foreground
+sources with a common far-field monopole boundary value. In local Cartesian
+components the nonlinear Hessian invariant is proportional to
+$(\operatorname{tr}H)^2-\operatorname{tr}(H^2)$, where $H$ is the
+Hessian of the scalar. Hence adding the two isolated scalar solutions
+is not generally the solution for the combined source. The ordinary
+foreground deflections are evaluated for the same prescribed density
+profiles and included once. Both scalar calculations use the same finite
+line-of-sight segment in the foreground plane. The comparison does not
+solve the material source from the BHSM state.
+
+\paragraph{Two-plane propagation.}
+Let $\boldsymbol{\theta}_g$ be the observed angular position of G1,
+$D_i$ and $D_{ij}$ angular diameter distances, and $\hat\alpha_j$
+physical bending angles in plane $j$. The G1 impact vector and inferred
+source position follow from the ordered lens map~\cite{Wong2017HE}:
+\begin{align}
+ \boldsymbol{x}_{2g}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) &=
+ D_2(\boldsymbol{\theta}-\boldsymbol{\theta}_g)
+ -D_{12}\big[\hat\alpha_1(D_1\boldsymbol{\theta})
+              -\hat\alpha_1(D_1\boldsymbol{\theta}_g)\big],\\
+ \boldsymbol{\beta}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) &=
+ \boldsymbol{\theta}
+ -\frac{D_{1s}}{D_s}\hat\alpha_1(D_1\boldsymbol{\theta})
+ -\frac{D_{2s}}{D_s}\hat\alpha_2(\boldsymbol{x}_{2g}).
+\end{align}
+The foreground map is applied to G1's observed light centroid as well
+as to the quasar rays. In the scalar comparison the same isolated G1
+response is used in both branches. With $\boldsymbol{\delta\beta}_i$
+the joint-minus-isolated shift at observed image $i$, the reported
+differential is $\max_{i,j}|\boldsymbol{\delta\beta}_i-
+\boldsymbol{\delta\beta}_j|$. This removes any common shift without
+permitting a different source position for each model image.
+
+\paragraph{Result and limit.}
+For the same four published image positions~\cite{Courbin2011}, the
+ordinary-only profiles give $0.898879''$ root-mean-square source-plane
+scatter about the four-ray centroid. The jointly solved R1 foreground
+and isolated R1 rear response give $0.898862''$. Across four accepted
+grid and box choices, the joint-minus-isolated scalar difference after
+G1 propagation changes the inferred separations by $4.66$--$4.81$
+microarcseconds on the same $\pm800$ kpc foreground path. Propagating the
+changed foreground through G1 contributes less than $0.02$
+microarcseconds to that differential. On the finest accepted grid
+($115\times229$, 1.5 Mpc half-box), increasing the path half-length
+from 800 to 1250 kpc changes the differential from 4.66 to 4.67
+microarcseconds. A finer widened-box run missed the declared
+$2\times10^{-7}$ scaled discrete residual target; it is retained in
+the replay output and excluded from the accepted range. Grid spread
+is a numerical sensitivity, not a physical uncertainty interval.
+
+The prescribed galaxy and environment thus produce a calculable
+foreground and a two-plane source map without using the quasar images
+to tune the density. But the four inferred sources are nowhere near a
+single point under this prescription. The small nonadditive term
+cannot close the missing-field gap. There is no physical time-delay
+prediction here: one common source and its stationary images are
+required before taking an arrival-time difference. The unresolved
+step is to specify the independently constrained total matter and
+environmental state (or derive an equivalent density from BHSM) and
+pass the common-source imaging test before confronting delays. The
+failure applies to the stated source prescription, not to all
+Berger--Hopf states or all conventional models.
+
+\subsection{A joint test of the field with HE~0435-1223 and Jackpot}
+\label{sec:joint-lens-terrain}
+The two systems constrain different aspects of the same proposed
+matter-and-environment to metric rule. HE~0435-1223 supplies angular
+constraints and relative arrival times for a variable source.
+Its extended host arcs improve the angular coverage but do not add
+a second source distance~\cite{Ding2017host}. SDSS~J0946+1006
+(Jackpot) has one principal deflector at $z=0.222$ and three
+multiply imaged sources at $z=(0.609,2.035,5.975)$
+\cite{CollettSmith2021Jackpot,Ballard2024Jackpot}. The angular
+and radial coverage of Jackpot complements the timing information
+of HE~0435-1223; the two systems must share a physical rule, not
+a fitted mass distribution or an unlensed source position.
+
+\paragraph{What the broad environment does in HE~0435-1223.}
+In the fixed-input geometry of the preceding calculation, switching on
+the associated group's projected field changes a pairwise source
+separation vector by up to $99.20$ milliarcseconds.
+Replacing that field with the quadratic Taylor expansion of its lensing
+potential at the main galaxy, with unchanged source and mass inputs and
+with the rear propagation repeated, changes the same pairwise diagnostic
+by only $1.01$ milliarcseconds. These are model comparisons,
+not measurements of a group signal. The smooth contribution matters
+here, but higher spatial variation of this group proxy does not
+account for the persistent $0.899''$ root-mean-square source scatter.
+
+\paragraph{What the additional distances test.}
+Let $W_{ji}=D_{ij}/D_j$ for $i<j$, and let $\widehat{\boldsymbol\alpha}_i$
+be the physical deflection on plane $i$. In the ordered thin-plane
+approximation, the ray angle in plane $j$ is
+\begin{equation}
+ \boldsymbol\theta_j=\boldsymbol\theta
+ -\sum_{i<j}W_{ji}\widehat{\boldsymbol\alpha}_i(D_i\boldsymbol\theta_i).
+ \label{eq:jackpot-terrain-transfer}
+\end{equation}
+The frozen R1 background gives primary deflection weights of
+$0.595$, $0.828$ and $0.892$ for the three Jackpot sources. The
+third source also has weights $0.732$ and $0.369$ for deflections
+at the first and second source planes, if those planes carry
+mass. A flat $\Lambda$CDM control with $\Omega_{\mathrm m}=0.315$
+gives primary weights $0.595$, $0.826$ and $0.889$. The weights alone
+do not determine the deflections: the images take different paths,
+and the intermediate deflectors cannot be absorbed into a mere
+change of source distance. In a published retrospective check, an
+earlier model using the first two sources mapped the two images of the
+third source to locations $0.15''$ apart in their source plane.
+Updating the second source redshift reduced that separation by
+$0.02''$, and adjustments within the earlier parameter uncertainties
+can reconcile the images~\cite{CollettSmith2021Jackpot}. This is
+neither a BHSM signal nor a failure of general relativity.
+
+\paragraph{First fixed-input calculation on Jackpot.}
+The two published images of the third source probe projected radii of
+$1.35''$ and $3.56''$~\cite{CollettSmith2020Jackpot}. We use only the
+main galaxy's two-component HST light profile and
+stellar-population masses~\cite{Sonnenfeld2012Jackpot}, with no
+mass, halo, or coupling adjusted to either image. For spherical
+area-preserving proxies, the two rays back-project to points
+$3.867''$ apart with the Chabrier stellar mass and $2.994''$ apart
+with the Salpeter stellar mass. The frozen, baryon-sourced spherical
+R1 term moves the separation vector by $62$ and $86$ microarcseconds,
+respectively. Retaining the measured stellar ellipticities and
+orientations in ordinary gravity gives separations of $3.888''$ and
+$3.032''$. This is a control for the stellar geometry, not a solution
+of the anisotropic R1 equation. Two intervening isothermal deflectors
+with assumed dispersions $100$ and $150$ km/s can change that vector
+by at most $0.901''$ in this control, regardless of their centers;
+this bound is specific to those profiles and dispersions, not to
+the unseen matter in the field. No posterior uncertainty is
+claimed. The published two-source model uses lensing information
+to constrain the total density. Its smaller residual is a benchmark,
+not a matched-input comparison. In both HE~0435-1223 and Jackpot,
+the tested starlight-led prescription plus the retained R1 response
+does not predict a field that brings the observed rays together.
+
+\paragraph{A criterion for adding knowledge.}
+Use a single independently specified BHSM rule to construct a field for
+each lens from that lens's own physical inputs. For Jackpot, freeze the
+rule and the deflector constraints without the third-source image
+positions. Back-project the two positions after freezing, and compare
+their separation with the propagated astrometric and model uncertainty.
+The image-plane likelihood or the covariance-weighted source statistic
+must be used so that a mere rescaling of source coordinates does not
+improve the score.
+Equivalently, use one image to specify the otherwise unknown source
+position and predict the other. Fit or marginalize the lower-redshift
+source light only under the same data and prior rules in both
+comparisons. For HE~0435-1223, require common-source closure before
+scoring withheld delay differences. Matched resolved host features
+can also test relative image distortions: for Jacobians $J_i$ mapping
+image to source, $J_j^{-1}J_i$ cancels a common source-coordinate
+Jacobian at the matched feature. Report the two results jointly,
+with no lens-specific adjustment of the hypothesized BHSM coupling
+after inspecting either target. A conventional field with the same
+effective potentials will reproduce the same optics. A BHSM addition
+would therefore be an independently fixed feature of the field that
+predicts the reserved observations, not a new label for the fitted
+potential. The geometry calculation sets up that test; it does not
+supply the missing BHSM environmental source law or a new observational
+fit to Jackpot.
+
 \section{Conclusions}
+The multi-source extension in Section~\ref{sec:joint-lens-terrain}
+sets a joint observational criterion: one fixed source-to-field rule
+must account for independent source tracks in Jackpot and relative
+arrival times in HE~0435-1223. The published Jackpot residual and
+the fixed-input closure failures motivate the test;
+neither establishes a BHSM preference.
+
+The principal result is a constructive ordinary-gravity
+correspondence. For the specified R1 branch, a positive conventional
+effective mass produces the same retained metric and optical transfer,
+hence the same image positions and relative arrival times. This
+equality is exact at the stated order. It does not imply that the
+retained model fits the observed lens to the claimed precision, nor is
+it asserted beyond the approximation used here.
