-\abstract{Prepared environmental light probes a state-conditioned optical transfer in the Berger--Hopf interpretation. We calculate a regular finite-body response from the specified R1 cubic scalar--tensor action, obtaining fixed-preparation delay offsets of 25.9593 s and 8.6490 s. A positive conventional effective mass reproduces the retained metric, limiting state identifiability. We construct an HE 0435-1223 observed-light baseline and test separately frozen predictions. Brightness undercoverage, conditional HST central-image tension and a 3.26 mas Gaia discrepancy motivate source and geometry revisions. A positive G1 perturber reduces consumed-Gaia coordinate RMS to about 0.011 mas without enlarging errors, but the new JWST test fails instrumental adequacy. Correcting native-rate eligibility restores 303 valid slopes and gives about 0.45 mas raw geometry RMS for both reference and R1-inclusive controls; all sixteen profiles still fail. Actual target histories and source calibration darks expose missing temporal and interpixel covariance. The coupled coherent-sky/response mean converges, but its held exposure remains inadequate. Calibration-ordered readout applications retain the original residual evidence rather than equating changed errors with validation. These development results establish neither precision validation nor a BHSM detection or unique envelope-state measurement.}
+\abstract{Why does the Berger--Hopf R1 lens give the same result as ordinary gravity? For the spherical R1 family at the order calculated, the screened scalar changes the two equal weak-field metric potentials in exactly the way a nonnegative conventional mass density would. Identical potentials give identical light deflections, images and relative arrival times. R1 adds a predictive relation: once its state and matter source are specified, the scalar field equation determines the additional mass profile and its dependence on scalar mixing and stiffness, rather than fitting a separate halo for the same response. This is an advantage in physical interpretation, not an improvement in fit. Holding the ordinary matter profile fixed instead gives conditional offsets of 25.9593 s and 8.6490 s in a synthetic lens, not measured delays in HE~0435-1223. In that observed lens, the withheld COSMOGRAIL and unused LCO photometry give nearly tied scores, and JWST instrumental adequacy checks fail, including an internal check that reuses an exposure. A fixed-input nonlinear galaxy-and-group field propagated through the rear G1 plane still leaves 0.899 arcseconds of source-plane scatter, so it provides no observed-lens delay prediction. The correspondence is exact within the retained approximation; the data do not establish a precision fit or a unique BHSM envelope state.}
